<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 21 L5. COUNT-IN: the click plays for N bars, THEN recording begins
  AT THE LOCATOR.

  THE READING TAKEN, because there are two and they differ by N bars of the
  user's recording. The count-in is BEFORE the record position (Cubase / Logic
  / REAPER): the playhead does not move during it, the click counts the bars in,
  and the take then begins exactly where the locator was. The rejected reading -
  roll the count-in bars ON the timeline, so the take lands N bars later - makes
  a preference silently move the recording.

  Env, on the CTest entry: RUN_SERIAL, SMARAGD_CAPTURE_SPEED=1,
  SMARAGD_AUDIO_INPUT_BACKEND=file:../test_position.wav,
  SMARAGD_AUDIO_INPUT_LATENCY_FRAMES=4800.

  THE CLOSED FORM. 120 BPM, 4/4, 48 kHz:

      beat = 24000 frames,  bar = 96000 frames = 2 s

  so TWO bars of count-in are EIGHT clicks, 24000 frames apart, with beats 1
  and 5 accented.
  It is EXACTLY eight and not "about eight" because the click carries a
  half-open ACTIVE RANGE `[locator, locator + countIn)` (twmetronome.h): the
  pump renders one to two blocks ahead of the RT, so a plain "stop clicking now"
  would always let the downbeat past the end through, and the count would be
  eight or nine depending on the box.

  THE LOCATOR IS 96000 (bar 3), not 0, so the placement assertion is about the
  count-in and not about a clamp at the start of the project.

  WHY THE ARMED TRACK'S MONITOR IS OFF. This case measures the CLICK in the
  capture, and a monitored input would put the position-encoded fixture in there
  as well - which is monitor_through_chain's job. With monitoring off the only
  live lane in this run is the count-in's, which is exactly the claim "a live
  lane exists iff armed u monitor u METRONOME".

  ...and the project's own metronome switch stays OFF, which is what makes the
  click count EXACT: the count-in clicks and nothing else does, so the capture
  holds eight clicks and then the take, with no click over the take at all.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="record_count_in">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <set-tempo bpm="120"/>
    <set-track-input trackPath="0" input="audio:file:1"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="0" mode="off"/>

    <!-- This case OWNS transport/countInBars and transport/preRollBars; both
         are put back at the end. -->
    <set-count-in bars="2"/>
    <set-pre-roll bars="0"/>

    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="1"/>
    <set-locator position="96000"/>

    <!-- record-start returns immediately: the count-in runs on the event loop
         (SApplication::pumpRecordPreamble) and the take begins when the RT has
         been handed two bars of click. -->
    <record-start/>
    <wait-ms ms="4700"/>
    <!-- ...by now the two-bar count-in is over and the take is running. -->
    <wait-ms ms="1400"/>
    <record-stop/>

    <!-- THE PLACEMENT: at the LOCATOR. The take started the transport, so the
         trim floor is the transport start (design D6) and the compensated
         placement is clamped up to it - which is what "the clip lands where you
         put the locator" means in the presence of latency compensation. -->
    <assert-recorded-clip trackPath="0" clips="1" passes="1"
                          startFrame="96000" startTolerance="2048"
                          inputLatencyFrames="4800"
                          outputLatencyFrames="1024"
                          userOffsetFrames="0"
                          compensationFrames="-5824"
                          minDurationFrames="24000"/>

    <!-- THE CLICKS: exactly EIGHT, on the beat, one in four louder, silence
         between them - and NOTHING after them, because the project's own
         metronome is off, so the take itself is clickless. Capture frame 0 is
         the device start, which the count-in itself caused, so the whole
         count-in is in this recording.

         EXACTLY eight is the claim worth making and it is only true because
         the click carries a half-open range AND stops before the transport
         does its start-of-run reposition (SLiveMonitor::muteCountIn) - without
         either of those the count is nine, with the extra one a replay of the
         first beat. -->
    <dump-playback-capture filename="countin.wav" minFrames="192000"/>
    <assert-metronome-clicks filename="countin.wav" channel="0"
                             threshold="0.05" window="64" minGapFrames="4800"
                             count="8"
                             intervalFrames="24000" toleranceFrames="1024"
                             accentEvery="4" accentRatio="1.5"
                             silenceMaxRms="0.01"/>

    <set-count-in bars="0"/>
    <assert-render-policy liveThreadRefusals="0" liveOwnedRefusals="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
